course: dictionary
lesson: 9220
title: Somali: a country, a people, a language
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one you need. «Somali» is one of those words.
explain: «The Somali people, for example, live in Somalia, Ethiopia, Kenya and Djibouti.» Here Somali means a people, the group of people who share a language and culture. Somali can also describe something, like Somali food or the Somali language.
explain: Look for clues. A country name, a word like language or food, or a capital letter can all help. Ask yourself: can I say "from Somalia" or "in the Somali language" and still make sense?
fact: Somalia is a country in the Horn of East Africa.
fact: «Somali» can describe something (Somali food), name a person (a Somali), or name a language (the Somali language).
fact: The Somali language is spoken by about 20 million people in Somalia, Djibouti, Ethiopia, Kenya and by Somalis around the world.
fact: Somalia has the longest coastline in mainland Africa.
fact: Many Somali families live in cities such as London, Bristol, Birmingham, Toronto and Minneapolis.
exercise: «The Somali people, for example, live in Somalia, Ethiopia, Kenya and Djibouti.» What does Somali mean in this sentence? | A cold wind from the sea | *A people, from Somalia and nearby lands | A very old castle | A kind of fruit
exercise: Amira says, «I am learning Somali at school.» What is Somali here? | A town in Kenya | *The language spoken in Somalia | A kind of boat | A type of sand
exercise: Which phrase means food from Somalia? | Somali bus | Somali sand | Somali coat | *Somali food
test: Which city has many Somali families living in it? | Tokyo | *London | Paris | Lagos
test: Which country has the longest coastline in mainland Africa? | Kenya | Djibouti | Egypt | *Somalia
exam: How can you tell which meaning of Somali is right in a sentence? | Ask a friend to guess | *Look at the words and clues around it | Count the letters in the word | Skip the sentence forever
